For each point `i`, count how many later points share each direction from it. The best line through `i` has `1 + (largest direction count)` points. The answer is the maximum over all `i`. This is O(n²) with n ≤ 300, which is fast enough.

**Representing a direction exactly.** For another point `j`, let `dx = xj - xi` and `dy = yj - yi`.

1. Divide both by `g = gcd(|dx|, |dy|)`.
2. Normalize the sign so the pair is unique:
   - If `dx < 0`, negate both.
   - If `dx == 0`, set `dy = 1` (vertical line).
3. Use the string `"dx,dy"` as the hash key.

Because the points are unique, `dx` and `dy` are never both zero, so `g > 0`.

**Edge case.** If there is only one point, the answer is 1.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $points
     * @return Integer
     */
    function maxPoints($points) {
        $n = count($points);
        if ($n <= 2) return $n;
        $best = 1;
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $map = [];
            $localMax = 0;
            for ($j = $i + 1; $j < $n; $j++) {
                $dx = $points[$j][0] - $points[$i][0];
                $dy = $points[$j][1] - $points[$i][1];
                $a = abs($dx); $b = abs($dy);
                while ($b != 0) { $t = $a % $b; $a = $b; $b = $t; }
                $g = $a;
                $dx = intdiv($dx, $g);
                $dy = intdiv($dy, $g);
                if ($dx < 0) { $dx = -$dx; $dy = -$dy; }
                elseif ($dx == 0) { $dy = 1; }
                $key = $dx . ',' . $dy;
                $map[$key] = ($map[$key] ?? 0) + 1;
                if ($map[$key] > $localMax) $localMax = $map[$key];
            }
            if ($localMax + 1 > $best) $best = $localMax + 1;
        }
        return $best;
    }
}
```

