traces.inference_trace:
Use a bitmask DP over the set of elements already removed.

1. **State.** `dp[mask]` is the best score after removing exactly the elements whose bits are set in `mask`.
2. **Operation index.** Since each operation removes two elements, the next operation number is `popcount(mask) / 2 + 1`.
3. **Transition.** From a reachable `mask` with an even popcount, pick any two unused elements `i < j`. Move to `mask | (1<<i) | (1<<j)` and add `op * gcd(nums[i], nums[j])`.
4. **Pairing all elements once.** Always fix `i` as the lowest unset bit and only vary `j`. Every pair still gets used eventually, so no optimal sequence is lost, and the number of transitions drops sharply.
5. **Answer.** `dp[full]`, where `full` has all `2n` bits set.

To avoid recomputation, the pairwise gcd values are precomputed into a table `g`. With at most 14 elements there are `2^14 = 16384` masks.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @return Integer
     */
    function maxScore($nums) {
        $m = count($nums);
        $g = [];
        for ($i = 0; $i < $m; $i++) {
            for ($j = $i + 1; $j < $m; $j++) {
                $a = $nums[$i]; $b = $nums[$j];
                while ($b) { $t = $a % $b; $a = $b; $b = $t; }
                $g[$i][$j] = $a;
            }
        }
        $full = (1 << $m) - 1;
        $dp = array_fill(0, $full + 1, -1);
        $dp[0] = 0;
        for ($mask = 0; $mask < $full; $mask++) {
            if ($dp[$mask] < 0) continue;
            $cnt = 0; $x = $mask;
            while ($x) { $cnt += $x & 1; $x >>= 1; }
            $op = intdiv($cnt, 2) + 1;
            $i = 0;
            while (($mask >> $i) & 1) $i++;
            for ($j = $i + 1; $j < $m; $j++) {
                if (($mask >> $j) & 1) continue;
                $nm = $mask | (1 << $i) | (1 << $j);
                $v = $dp[$mask] + $op * $g[$i][$j];
                if ($v > $dp[$nm]) $dp[$nm] = $v;
            }
        }
        return $dp[$full];
    }
}
```

