With `word.length <= 100`, an O(n²) brute force is fast enough.

1. For each start index `i`, extend the substring to the right one character at a time.
2. Keep a set of the distinct vowels seen so far.
3. Stop extending as soon as a non-vowel appears, since no longer substring from `i` can be valid.
4. After adding each vowel, if the set holds all five vowels, count that substring.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $word
     * @return Integer
     */
    function countVowelSubstrings($word) {
        $n = strlen($word);
        $vowels = ['a' => true, 'e' => true, 'i' => true, 'o' => true, 'u' => true];
        $count = 0;
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $seen = [];
            for ($j = $i; $j < $n; $j++) {
                $c = $word[$j];
                if (!isset($vowels[$c])) break;
                $seen[$c] = true;
                if (count($seen) == 5) $count++;
            }
        }
        return $count;
    }
}
```

