The two strings are close exactly when three conditions hold:

1. **Same length.** Neither operation changes the length of a string.
2. **Same set of characters.** Both operations only use existing characters, so no new letter can appear and no letter can disappear.
3. **Same multiset of frequencies.** Operation 2 swaps which letters carry which counts, and operation 1 reorders positions. Together they let any count be reassigned to any existing letter. So the sorted list of counts must match.

To check this, count characters in each string with `count_chars($s, 1)`, which returns only the characters that appear.
- Compare the key sets with `array_keys` after sorting.
- Compare the sorted values to check the frequency multisets.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $word1
     * @param String $word2
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function closeStrings($word1, $word2) {
        if (strlen($word1) != strlen($word2)) return false;
        $c1 = count_chars($word1, 1);
        $c2 = count_chars($word2, 1);
        $k1 = array_keys($c1);
        $k2 = array_keys($c2);
        sort($k1);
        sort($k2);
        if ($k1 !== $k2) return false;
        $v1 = array_values($c1);
        $v2 = array_values($c2);
        sort($v1);
        sort($v2);
        return $v1 === $v2;
    }
}
```

