Track two maps while scanning the prefix:
- `cnt[x]`: how many times number `x` has appeared.
- `freq[f]`: how many distinct numbers currently appear exactly `f` times.

Also keep `maxf`, the largest count so far.

For a prefix of length `len = i+1`, removing one element works in exactly three cases:

1. **`maxf == 1`**: every number appears once, so removing any one leaves all counts equal.
2. **`freq[maxf] * maxf + 1 == len`**: all numbers have count `maxf` except one number with count 1. Removing that single occurrence leaves all counts equal.
3. **`freq[maxf-1] * (maxf-1) + maxf == len`**: one number has count `maxf` and all others have `maxf-1`. Removing one occurrence of the top number equalizes them. This also covers a single distinct number, where `freq[maxf-1]` is 0 and `maxf == len`.

The last index where any case holds gives the answer `i+1`.

**Checking case 3 when `maxf == 1`:** the term is `freq[0] * 0 + 1 == len`, which holds only when `len == 1`. That is fine, and case 1 already covers it.

**Checking against the examples:**
- Example 2: at `len = 13`, `freq[3] = 4` and `4 * 3 + 1 = 13`, so case 2 applies.
- Example 1: at `len = 7`, the counts are 2, 2, 1, 2, so `freq[2] = 3` and `3 * 2 + 1 = 7`, so case 2 applies.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @return Integer
     */
    function maxEqualFreq($nums) {
        $cnt = [];
        $freq = [];
        $maxf = 0;
        $ans = 0;
        $n = count($nums);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $x = $nums[$i];
            $c = $cnt[$x] ?? 0;
            if ($c > 0) {
                $freq[$c]--;
            }
            $c++;
            $cnt[$x] = $c;
            $freq[$c] = ($freq[$c] ?? 0) + 1;
            if ($c > $maxf) $maxf = $c;
            $len = $i + 1;
