Use a sliding window over the sorted fruit positions. Any window `[L, R]` can be fully harvested by visiting one end first and then the other.

The cost of covering `[L, R]` from `startPos` is:

- If `R <= startPos`: cost is `startPos - L`.
- If `L >= startPos`: cost is `R - startPos`.
- Otherwise: cost is `(R - L) + min(startPos - L, R - startPos)`.

This single formula covers all three cases:

`cost = (R - L) + min(|startPos - L|, |R - startPos|)`

Check each case:

- If `R <= startPos`: `(R - L) + (startPos - R) = startPos - L`. This is the min term, since `startPos - R <= startPos - L`.
- If `L >= startPos`: `(R - L) + (L - startPos) = R - startPos`. This is also correct.

Algorithm:

1. Move the right pointer `r` forward, adding `fruits[r][1]` to the running sum.
2. While the cost of the window `[fruits[l][0], fruits[r][0]]` exceeds `k`, subtract `fruits[l][1]` and advance `l`.
3. Record the maximum sum seen.

When `l > r`, the window is empty with sum 0. The cost check only runs while `l <= r`. A single point far away has cost `|startPos - p|`, so if that exceeds `k`, `l` passes `r` and the sum drops to 0.

Both pointers only move forward, so this runs in O(n).

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $fruits
     * @param Integer $startPos
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Integer
     */
    function maxTotalFruits($fruits, $startPos, $k) {
        $n = count($fruits);
        $l = 0;
        $sum = 0;
        $best = 0;
        for ($r = 0; $r < $n; $r++) {
            $sum += $fruits[$r][1];
            while ($l <= $r) {
                $L = $fruits[$l][0];
                $R = $fruits[$r][0];
                $cost = ($R - $L) + min(abs($startPos - $L), abs($R - $startPos));
                if ($cost <= $k) break;
                $sum -= $fruits[$l][1];
                $l++;
            }
            if ($sum > $best) $best = $sum;
        }
        return $best;
    }
}
```

