Walk through the array once. Keep a pointer `start` for the first index not yet added to the result.

Each time `nums[j] == key`:
- The indices within distance `k` of `j` run from `j - k` to `j + k`, clipped to the array bounds.
- Begin at `max(start, j - k)` so no index is added twice.
- Add every index up to `min(n - 1, j + k)`.
- Move `start` past the last index added.

Indices are appended in increasing order, so no sorting is needed. Each index is added at most once, so the total work is O(n).

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @param Integer $key
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function findKDistantIndices($nums, $key, $k) {
        $n = count($nums);
        $res = [];
        $start = 0;
        for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j++) {
            if ($nums[$j] == $key) {
                $from = max($start, $j - $k);
                $to = min($n - 1, $j + $k);
                for ($i = $from; $i <= $to; $i++) {
                    $res[] = $i;
                }
                $start = max($start, $to + 1);
            }
        }
        return $res;
    }
}
