Approach: run Kahn's algorithm on the reversed graph. A node is safe exactly when every path from it ends at a terminal node.

1. Build the reverse adjacency list: for each edge `i -> j`, record `i` in `rev[j]`.
2. Set `outdeg[i]` to the number of outgoing edges of node `i`.
3. Put every node with `outdeg 0` (the terminal nodes) into a queue.
4. Pop a node `v` and mark it safe. For each predecessor `u` in `rev[v]`, decrement `outdeg[u]`. If it reaches 0, all of `u`'s edges lead to safe nodes, so push `u`.
5. Nodes on or leading into a cycle never reach `outdeg 0`, so they are never marked.
6. Collect the safe nodes in index order, which gives the ascending output directly.

This avoids recursion, so deep graphs cannot overflow the stack. It runs in O(n + e) time and space.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $graph
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function eventualSafeNodes($graph) {
        $n = count($graph);
        $rev = array_fill(0, $n, []);
        $outdeg = array_fill(0, $n, 0);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $outdeg[$i] = count($graph[$i]);
            foreach ($graph[$i] as $j) {
                $rev[$j][] = $i;
            }
        }
        $queue = [];
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            if ($outdeg[$i] == 0) $queue[] = $i;
        }
        $safe = array_fill(0, $n, false);
        $head = 0;
        while ($head < count($queue)) {
            $v = $queue[$head++];
            $safe[$v] = true;
            foreach ($rev[$v] as $u) {
                if (--$outdeg[$u] == 0) {
                    $queue[] = $u;
                }
            }
        }
        $res = [];
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            if ($safe[$i]) $res[] = $i;
        }
        return $res;
    }
}
