Approach: put the broken letters in a lookup set, then split the text on spaces and count the words that contain none of those letters.

1. Build an associative array `$broken` whose keys are the broken letters, so each membership check is constant time.
2. Split `$text` with `explode(' ', ...)`. Empty pieces are skipped in case of stray spaces, like the trailing spaces shown in the examples.
3. For each word, scan its characters. If any character is in `$broken`, the word cannot be typed. Otherwise, increment the count.

Running time is O(n), where n is the length of `text`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $text
     * @param String $brokenLetters
     * @return Integer
     */
    function canBeTypedWords($text, $brokenLetters) {
        $broken = [];
        $bl = strlen($brokenLetters);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $bl; $i++) {
            $c = $brokenLetters[$i];
            if ($c >= 'a' && $c <= 'z') $broken[$c] = true;
        }
        $count = 0;
        foreach (explode(' ', $text) as $w) {
            if ($w === '') continue;
            $ok = true;
            $n = strlen($w);
            for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
                if (isset($broken[$w[$i]])) { $ok = false; break; }
            }
            if ($ok) $count++;
        }
        return $count;
    }
}
```

