traces.inference_trace:
Approach: dynamic programming over words processed in increasing length order.

1. Sort the words by length, so every possible predecessor is handled before the longer word that uses it.
2. Keep a map `dp[word]` = length of the longest chain ending at `word`.
3. For each word, try deleting each character in turn. If the resulting shorter string is already in `dp`, the chain can be extended: `dp[word] = max(dp[word], dp[pred] + 1)`.
4. Each word starts at 1 (a single word is a chain), and the answer is the largest value seen.

Cost: at most 1000 words × 16 deletions, each building a string of length ≤ 16, which is small.

Edge case: PHP array keys that look like integer strings would be converted to ints. Lowercase letters never trigger this, but the empty string `''` can appear when deleting from a 1-letter word. Using `isset` on `''` is fine, and `''` is never stored as a key.

Duplicate words are harmless: the second copy just recomputes the same value.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String[] $words
     * @return Integer
     */
    function longestStrChain($words) {
        usort($words, function($a, $b) { return strlen($a) - strlen($b); });
        $dp = [];
        $best = 0;
        foreach ($words as $w) {
            $cur = 1;
            $n = strlen($w);
            for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
                $p = substr($w, 0, $i) . substr($w, $i + 1);
                if (isset($dp[$p]) && $dp[$p] + 1 > $cur) {
                    $cur = $dp[$p] + 1;
                }
            }
            if (!isset($dp[$w]) || $dp[$w] < $cur) $dp[$w] = $cur;
            if ($cur > $best) $best = $cur;
        }
        return $best;
    }
}
```

