traces.inference_trace:
Binary search on the number of days.

1. If `m * k > n`, there are not enough flowers, so return -1. In PHP, `m * k` is at most 1e11, which fits in a 64-bit integer.
2. Search the day `d` between the minimum and maximum of `bloomDay`. The answer always lies in that range once step 1 passes.
3. To test a day `d`, scan the flowers left to right and count consecutive bloomed flowers (`bloomDay[i] <= d`). Each time the run reaches `k`, form a bouquet and reset the run. Any unbloomed flower also resets the run.
4. Return the smallest `d` that yields at least `m` bouquets.

The check can stop early once the bouquet count reaches `m`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $bloomDay
     * @param Integer $m
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Integer
     */
    function minDays($bloomDay, $m, $k) {
        $n = count($bloomDay);
        if ($m * $k > $n) return -1;
        $lo = min($bloomDay);
        $hi = max($bloomDay);
        while ($lo < $hi) {
            $mid = intdiv($lo + $hi, 2);
            $cnt = 0; $run = 0;
            foreach ($bloomDay as $b) {
                if ($b <= $mid) {
                    $run++;
                    if ($run == $k) { $cnt++; $run = 0; if ($cnt >= $m) break; }
                } else {
                    $run = 0;
                }
            }
            if ($cnt >= $m) $hi = $mid;
            else $lo = $mid + 1;
        }
        return $lo;
    }
}
```

