traces.inference_trace:
Approach: bitmask DP over subsets of `nums`.

1. Let `sum` be the total of `nums`. If `sum % k != 0`, no equal split exists. Otherwise the target per subset is `t = sum / k`. If any single element is larger than `t`, return false.
2. Sort `nums`. Define `dp[mask]` as the running sum of the current (partially filled) subset after using exactly the elements in `mask`, i.e. the total of those elements modulo `t`. It is -1 if `mask` is unreachable.
3. Start with `dp[0] = 0`. For each reachable `mask`, try adding each unused element `i`:
   - If `dp[mask] + nums[i] <= t`, mark `dp[mask | 1<<i] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % t`.
   - Because `nums` is sorted, once `dp[mask] + nums[i] > t`, every later element is also too large, so break.
4. The answer is true exactly when `dp[full] == 0`, meaning every element was used and each subset closed at exactly `t`.

Complexity: 2^16 masks × 16 elements ≈ 1M operations, which is fine in PHP. Using `SplFixedArray` keeps the DP table compact.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function canPartitionKSubsets($nums, $k) {
        $sum = array_sum($nums);
        if ($sum % $k != 0) return false;
        $t = intdiv($sum, $k);
        sort($nums);
        $n = count($nums);
        if ($nums[$n - 1] > $t) return false;
        $full = (1 << $n) - 1;
        $dp = new SplFixedArray($full + 1);
        for ($i = 0; $i <= $full; $i++) $dp[$i] = -1;
        $dp[0] = 0;
        for ($mask = 0; $mask <= $full; $mask++) {
            $cur = $dp[$mask];
            if ($cur < 0) continue;
            for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
                if ($mask & (1 << $i)) continue;
                $s = $cur + $nums[$i];
                if ($s > $t) break;
                $nm = $mask | (1 << $i);
                if ($dp[$nm] < 0) {
                    $dp[$nm] = $s % $t;
                }
            }
        }
        return $dp[$full] == 0;
    }
}
```

