traces.inference_trace:
Binary search on the answer, the largest allowed subarray sum.

- The answer lies between `max(nums)` (every element must fit in some subarray) and `sum(nums)` (one subarray holds everything).
- For a candidate limit `mid`, scan the array greedily. Start a new subarray whenever adding the next element would push the current sum past `mid`, and count the pieces.
- If the count is at most `k`, then `mid` is feasible. Any split into fewer pieces can be refined into exactly `k` non-empty pieces without raising the largest sum, which is possible because `k <= n`.
- Feasibility is monotonic in `mid`, so we search for the smallest feasible value.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Integer
     */
    function splitArray($nums, $k) {
        $lo = max($nums);
        $hi = array_sum($nums);
        while ($lo < $hi) {
            $mid = intdiv($lo + $hi, 2);
            $cnt = 1;
            $cur = 0;
            foreach ($nums as $x) {
                if ($cur + $x > $mid) {
                    $cnt++;
                    $cur = $x;
                } else {
                    $cur += $x;
                }
            }
            if ($cnt <= $k) {
                $hi = $mid;
            } else {
                $lo = $mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return $lo;
    }
}
```

