Treat the pairs as edges of a path graph and walk it from one end.

1. **Build adjacency lists.** For each pair `[u, v]`, add `v` to `u`'s list and `u` to `v`'s list.
2. **Find an endpoint.** The first and last elements of `nums` each have exactly one neighbor. Every other element has two. Pick any element whose list has size 1 as the start.
3. **Walk the path.** From the start, repeatedly move to the neighbor that is not the previous element, until all `n = count(pairs) + 1` elements are placed.
   - The second element is the start's only neighbor.
   - For each later element, look at the current element's two neighbors and take the one different from the element before it.

PHP arrays accept negative integer keys, so values like `-100000` can be used directly as keys.

This runs in O(n) time and O(n) space.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $adjacentPairs
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function restoreArray($adjacentPairs) {
        $adj = [];
        foreach ($adjacentPairs as $p) {
            $adj[$p[0]][] = $p[1];
            $adj[$p[1]][] = $p[0];
        }
        $start = null;
        foreach ($adj as $k => $list) {
            if (count($list) == 1) {
                $start = $k;
                break;
            }
        }
        $n = count($adjacentPairs) + 1;
        $res = [$start];
        $prev = $start;
        $cur = $adj[$start][0];
        $res[] = $cur;
        for ($i = 2; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $nb = $adj[$cur];
            $next = ($nb[0] == $prev) ? $nb[1] : $nb[0];
            $prev = $cur;
            $cur = $next;
            $res[] = $cur;
        }
        return $res;
    }
}
