traces.inference_trace:
Use the greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the most recent `'*'`. It runs in O(n·m) worst case and usually much faster.

**Pointers and state**
- `i` walks through `s`, `j` walks through `p`.
- `star` is the position of the last `'*'` seen in `p` (initially -1).
- `match` is the position in `s` where that `'*'` started matching.

**Loop while `i < n`:**
- If `p[j]` is `'?'` or equals `s[i]`, advance both `i` and `j`.
- Otherwise, if `p[j]` is `'*'`:
  - set `star = j` and `match = i`;
  - advance only `j`, so the `'*'` initially matches the empty sequence.
- Otherwise, if a previous `'*'` exists:
  - let it absorb one more character: `j = star + 1`, `match++`, `i = match`.
- Otherwise, return false.

**After the loop:** skip any trailing `'*'` in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` has reached the end of `p`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $s
     * @param String $p
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function isMatch($s, $p) {
        $n = strlen($s);
        $m = strlen($p);
        $i = 0; $j = 0;
        $star = -1; $match = 0;
        while ($i < $n) {
            if ($j < $m && ($p[$j] === '?' || $p[$j] === $s[$i])) {
                $i++; $j++;
            } elseif ($j < $m && $p[$j] === '*') {
                $star = $j;
                $match = $i;
                $j++;
            } elseif ($star !== -1) {
                $j = $star + 1;
                $match++;
                $i = $match;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while ($j < $m && $p[$j] === '*') {
            $j++;
        }
        return $j === $m;
    }
}
```

