Keep an array `tails`, where `tails[k]` is the smallest possible last element of a strictly increasing subsequence of length `k + 1`.

For each number `x` in `nums`:
- Binary-search for the first index whose value is `>= x` (the lower bound).
- If no such index exists, append `x`, since it extends the longest subsequence found so far.
- Otherwise, replace the value at that index with `x`, which keeps that tail as small as possible.

Using lower bound (`>= x`) rather than upper bound keeps the subsequence strictly increasing, so equal values never extend it. The answer is the final length of `tails`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @return Integer
     */
    function lengthOfLIS($nums) {
        $tails = [];
        $len = 0;
        foreach ($nums as $x) {
            $lo = 0;
            $hi = $len;
            while ($lo < $hi) {
                $mid = ($lo + $hi) >> 1;
                if ($tails[$mid] < $x) {
                    $lo = $mid + 1;
                } else {
                    $hi = $mid;
                }
            }
            $tails[$lo] = $x;
            if ($lo == $len) {
                $len++;
            }
        }
        return $len;
    }
}
```

